# Every document agrees, and the credit **still** is not claimable for that
# period — because the goods had not arrived yet. Section 16(2)(b), Plan 108
# Slice 8.
#
# **This is the only rule in the pack that is not a document comparison, and
# the distinction is the point.** Slices 1–5 all ask "does a filing contain
# this invoice" — a presence/absence join across named graphs. This one asks
# "has something happened yet". An invoice can pass every one of those checks
# — booked, declared in GSTR-1, present in the right period's GSTR-2B — and
# still carry no claimable credit, because Section 16(2) requires *all* of its
# conditions to be satisfied and "the documents agree" is only one of them.
# Folding this into the matching rules would hide exactly the case it exists
# to catch.
#
# **The comparison is period-to-period, not date-to-date.** ITC is claimed in
# a return period, so a receipt on 4 September against an August 2B is a
# finding, and a receipt on 31 August against the same 2B is not — one day
# apart, opposite answers, and a naive "receipt after the invoice date" test
# gets both wrong. `SUBSTR(?receivedAt, 1, 7)` is the receipt's own period,
# which is comparable with `?period` only because every date in this pack is
# normalized to ISO-8601 at import (`isoDate` in the importers) so that
# lexicographic order *is* chronological order. A day-first date passed
# through unconverted would silently sort by day-of-month and this rule would
# report the wrong months while looking entirely authoritative.
#
# **`?period` is the 2B document's own return period**, which is the period the
# credit was made available in — not the invoice's date. The two differ in
# every carry-forward case, which is the case this pack most needs to be right
# about.
#
# **An invoice with no `GoodsReceipt` at all does not fire this rule**, and
# that is deliberate rather than an oversight. "Received later" and "never
# recorded as received" are different findings with different next actions —
# one is a timing question, the other is a data-completeness one — and Plan
# 108's parking lot keeps the second as its own slice rather than letting this
# rule quietly mean both.

# **The register and GSTR-2B sides are joined through a shared canonical
# `gst:Invoice` subject — Plan 109 Slice 2** — and `?period` has moved off
# the per-line `Gstr2bInvoice` record onto its `Gstr2bStatement`, reached
# through `reflectedIn`'s second-level reuse.

# **`taxAmount` is bound and projected for the same reason
# `payment-overdue.sparql` carries it — Plan 123 Slice D.** s.16(2)(b) credit
# that must wait for the goods is a deduction on a working paper, and a
# deduction nobody can size is not actionable. Found live, alongside the Rule
# 37 case: the GSTR-3B working paper had no amount to subtract while this rule
# was reporting findings. The invoice is already bound, so carrying the amount
# costs nothing.

PREFIX gst: <https://graph-owl.dev/packs/gst#>

SELECT ?purchase ?number ?gstin ?taxAmount ?period ?receivedAt
WHERE {
  GRAPH ?register {
    ?canonical gst:recordedIn ?purchase .
    ?purchase a gst:PurchaseInvoice ;
              gst:issuedBy ?supplier ;
              gst:invoiceNumber ?number ;
              gst:taxAmount     ?taxAmount .
    ?supplier gst:supplierGstin ?gstin .
  }

  GRAPH ?authority {
    ?canonical gst:reflectedIn ?filed .
    ?filed a gst:Gstr2bInvoice ;
           gst:reflectedIn ?statement .
    ?statement gst:period ?period .
  }

  GRAPH ?receipts {
    ?receipt a gst:GoodsReceipt ;
             gst:onInvoice ?purchase ;
             gst:atTime    ?receivedAt .
  }

  FILTER (SUBSTR(?receivedAt, 1, 7) > ?period)
}
ORDER BY ?number
